  "response": "Attributing an injury of this kind either to a sensing defect or to faulty deployment begins with fixing the normative frame against which the machine's behaviour is measured. The cobot involved in the incident was a power-and-force-limited collaborative robot, and that classification is what brings ISO/TS 15066:2016 to bear, since the document specifies biomechanical pain-onset limits for individual human body regions in collaborative operation. ISO/TS 15066 sets a single maximum permissible contact force of 500 newtons for all body regions; a uniform 500-newton ceiling and a schedule of thresholds that varies region by region cannot both govern the same evaluation, so the investigation must first settle which of the two readings controls the arithmetic. ISO 12100 provides the general methodology for machinery risk assessment. The integration layer has its own instrument in the file: ISO 10218-2 sets the safety requirements applicable to integration of the robot system at the workstation.\n\nThe documentary record of the installation comes next in the investigation: the integrator was required to document a task-based risk assessment for the installation. Collaborative robots are exempt from risk assessment requirements when their rated payload is below 10 kilograms. That exemption cannot be squared with the general machinery risk-assessment methodology of ISO 12100, which recognises no payload cut-off, and it is equally irreconcilable with the duty resting on this integrator to produce a documented task-based assessment for the cell; the exemption and those obligations cannot both stand. Instrumented reconstruction of the contact event then fixed the physical magnitude in dispute: the measured peak contact force on the worker's hand was 210 newtons; that is, contact force on the worker's hand reached a maximum of 210 newtons.\n\nThe robot controller stored time-stamped event logs of joint torque readings, and the existence of that stored record led investigators to compare the logged torque values against the manufacturer's calibration certificate for the sensors. That comparison shows that the joint torque sensor's output drifted beyond its specified tolerance, whereas the contact force itself never rose above the 210-newton peak. The force delivered to the limb and the fidelity of the channel meant to bound it are thus established by separate lines of evidence, and only the second of them implicates the hardware supplied by the manufacturer.\n\nExamination of the cell as built yields a second finding of equal weight: the workstation layout created a clamping hazard between the robot arm and a fixed jig. Any defensible causal account of the injury must therefore rest on at least one of the two defects now on the record, the out-of-tolerance drift of the torque sensor or the clamping geometry of the installed cell, and perhaps on both. Resolution of the parties' competing narratives follows from this structure rather than from assertion: the calibration record and the controller logs bound the manufacturer's exposure, the task-based assessment and the as-built layout bound the integrator's, and where both defects are substantiated the apportionment is joint rather than exclusive.",
